### Subcommands as OOP
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

You don't always need real classes.  The [`subcommands` interface]({{_}}/subcommands)
takes a `namespace` keyword that accepts **several space-separated namespaces, searched in order**;
that search order behaves exactly like a method-resolution order.  So you can (ab)use it to fake
OOP with zero metaprogramming[^mro]: treat each namespace as a "class", list them most-derived first,
and you get **override** and **inheritance** for free.

The `pet` CLI below lists three namespaces, most-derived (puppy) to base (animal).  The puppy
namespace overrides `speak`, `fetch` is inherited from the dog namespace, and `legs` and `describe`
are inherited from the base animal namespace.

{{site.embed_mirror('demos/oop-subcommand.mk', 'demos/cmk/oop-subcommand.cmk', github=github, default='left')}}

Overridden, inherited, and inherited-parametric, in that order:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./demos/oop-subcommand.mk pet speak
yip!
$ ./demos/oop-subcommand.mk pet fetch
fetches the ball
$ ./demos/oop-subcommand.mk pet describe rex
rex is a kind of animal
```

